::ingredients

(green cardamom) 4
(black cardamom) 2
(coriander) 1 tbsp
(cloves) 2
(cumin) 1 tbsp
(fennel seeds) 1 tbsp
(bayleaf) 5/6, as written in source
(black pepper pods) 5/6, as written in source
(star anise, optional) 1
(mace, optional) no more than 3 strands

::equipment

(frying pan, heavy bottom)
(mixer)
(sieve)
(glass jars, airtight)

::instructions

{spices} = (green cardamom) + (black cardamom) + (coriander) + (cloves) + (cumin) + (fennel seeds) + (bayleaf) + (black pepper pods)
Use only as many cloves as you want for heat; other spice amounts may be adjusted to taste.
Optional [
    {spices} + (star anise)
]
Optional [
    {spices} + (mace)
    Do not exceed the recommended amount; too much makes the mixture bitter.
]
{spices} <wash, very well>
[
    In a very sunny place:
    {spices} <sun dry, ~1 day>
]
-OR-
[
    Otherwise:
    {spices} <place, in (frying pan)>
        <dry roast> ?= aromatic
]
{spices} <cool>
    <put, in (mixer)>
    <mix, thoroughly>
{powder} <sieve, with (sieve)>
Repeat [
    Put the remaining large pieces back into (mixer) and mix again, then sieve again.
]
until everything is a fine powder
{garam masala} <store, in (glass jars)>

::notes

The source normally omits star anise and mace except when making biriyani; source link: /lamb-biriyani.
The source gives storage as about 2/3 months in a cool dry place; slash notation is retained without interpreting it as a fraction or range.
# The source describes mace as a dried flower; that botanical claim has not been validated here.

